  "domain": "blockchain",
  "definition": "A cryptographic signature is a mathematical construction that allows a signing party to attach an unforgeable, verifiable proof of origin and integrity to a message or transaction, providing both authenticity and non-repudiation. Signatures are computed from the message content and the signer's private key using an asymmetric cryptographic algorithm; any party possessing the corresponding public key can independently verify the signature without learning the private key. Common schemes include ECDSA, Ed25519, and RSA-PSS, each with distinct performance and security profiles relevant to their deployment context.",
